  "notes": "Tool-calling eval (Layer A) for the agent's decide() step: given a viewer state + optional history + request, did it pick an ACCEPTABLE tool AND emit valid args? `accept` is a SET of acceptable tools, not one golden answer. The tight cases (circle-here, over-search, history) measure the real decision boundaries; the generous ones avoid punishing correct behavior. Pages are grounded in the repair-question gold spans so the injected page text is real procedure content. Smoke/regression gate, partly subjective. TWO-PAGE SPREAD: the viewer shows a spread, so each on-page scenario sets viewer.pages (the harness mirrors production — the active viewer.page leads, capped at 2). The answer is deliberately put on the LEADING page in some scenarios and the TRAILING page in others, so a 'just circle the first page' bias would fail. ARG VALIDATION (`expect`): the parts of a call we can check deterministically — a circle must land on a page actually on screen, and `expect.page` pins the exact page the target/destination is on (circle.page or go_to_page.page must equal it). search queries / done messages are free text → not arg-checked. NOTE: go_to_section was removed; chapter-name requests route to search. Categories: circle-here (target on a shown page → circle), chapter-search (request names a chapter → search), search (off-page part/procedure), over-search (already on the right page → must NOT search), history (reference resolved from past turns), navigate (explicit/relative page move → go_to_page; relative moves from a SPREAD: 'go back a page' = the page before the first shown page, 'next page' = the page after the last shown page), stuck (anti-loop regression cases built from real frustrating traces: the model parks on a page and keeps re-circling the same thing while the mechanic changes topic, rejects the page, or navigates — it MUST move (search/navigate/done), NOT circle the same page/target again), diagram-shown (the WHOLE diagram the mechanic asked to see is already on screen → the page IS the answer, reply done; must NOT circle a single sub-component out of a diagram they asked for whole), not-found (absent thing), coincidence (a search landed on a topically-WRONG page that merely shares a word with the request — e.g. 'second gear grinding' landing on the fuel-system WGT-actuator page whose parts table lists a part literally named 'Second gear' — so the model must NOT circle that coincidental match; it should search again with a better query, or done), post-search (a search landed on the RIGHT page where the target really is → circle it; the control that proves a coincidence-gate does not also suppress legitimate post-search circling). POST-SEARCH SCENARIOS (`landed`): a scenario with `landed: {page}` is graded on the decision AFTER a search lands a page — the harness skips the fresh state_message and instead feeds search_result_message for that page (the model's prior search sits in history). This is the only way to exercise the circle-vs-search choice at the post-search turn; ordinary scenarios test the fresh-viewer turn.",
